1
00:00:15,022 --> 00:00:16,183
[SPEAKER_00]: So, about ordination.

2
00:00:17,984 --> 00:00:28,750
[SPEAKER_00]: How necessary is it to officially ordain as a Buddhist monk in order to practice the dhamma?

3
00:00:32,512 --> 00:00:33,872
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in itself, it's not.

4
00:00:35,553 --> 00:00:43,818
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessary to go through like the formal ordination and acceptance and so on.

5
00:00:45,102 --> 00:00:47,064
[SPEAKER_01]: What is necessary is the lifestyle.

6
00:00:48,485 --> 00:01:01,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the non... the lifestyle that's free from householder's concerns, let's put it that way, that's necessary for like a complete liberation from suffering.

7
00:01:02,588 --> 00:01:15,351
[SPEAKER_01]: So, of course, there's a greater likelihood you're going to find the suitable or have access to a suitable environment for practice that's free from household life concerns, if you're a monk or a nun.

8
00:01:17,471 --> 00:01:31,074
[SPEAKER_01]: At least, theoretically, you have a better chance, because the ordained lifestyle, generally, into like a, say, the practice tradition, not just like a temple tradition,

9
00:01:33,978 --> 00:01:38,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Is better than, you know, crowded and dusty lifestyle of a householder.

10
00:01:39,402 --> 00:01:42,464
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that's not necessarily so.

11
00:01:43,825 --> 00:01:57,595
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense, you might ordain, you might join a community and be equally busy and full of daily concerns about, you know, running the temple and duties and all sorts of stuff that can then seem justified in doing.

12
00:01:58,466 --> 00:02:01,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, oh, well, look, this is all for the greater good of dhamma or the monastery.

13
00:02:02,027 --> 00:02:10,849
[SPEAKER_01]: But in reality, the motivation is the same as it is of a householder who is just, you know, going, being busy around and just doing random stuff to keep himself occupied.

14
00:02:12,310 --> 00:02:15,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's pretty much the distinction that a person needs to make.

15
00:02:15,631 --> 00:02:15,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Will...

16
00:02:19,063 --> 00:02:22,384
[SPEAKER_01]: If I were to ordain, would that improve my circumstances?

17
00:02:22,884 --> 00:02:31,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Would that provide better grounds for keeping the vinaya, better seclusion, more time for reflection?

18
00:02:32,846 --> 00:02:36,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Or would it sort of not really meet those requirements?

19
00:02:37,087 --> 00:02:44,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes you might not be able to find something that would be so, like, black and white, good or bad.

20
00:02:44,569 --> 00:02:47,490
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be something in between, such as, well, you know,

21
00:02:48,487 --> 00:02:56,631
[SPEAKER_01]: He would not... he's not providing as much seclusion as I would want, he's not providing as much as basis, but he has potential to do so more than my current living situation.

22
00:02:56,652 --> 00:02:58,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, yeah, then it's still better to ordain.

23
00:02:59,913 --> 00:03:03,575
[SPEAKER_01]: But in and of itself, wearing robes or whatever makes... really makes no difference.

24
00:03:04,256 --> 00:03:05,556
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just an external statement.

25
00:03:05,596 --> 00:03:05,997
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

26
00:03:06,157 --> 00:03:07,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally, it makes no difference.

27
00:03:09,479 --> 00:03:09,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

28
00:03:11,440 --> 00:03:17,463
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I was thinking, like, what's really necessary is

29
00:03:18,213 --> 00:03:25,416
[SPEAKER_00]: Going forth, as in being prepared to leave your... the household life.

30
00:03:25,656 --> 00:03:25,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

31
00:03:25,917 --> 00:03:26,717
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... Yeah.

32
00:03:30,679 --> 00:03:38,582
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm wondering if that necessarily sort of implies, yeah, wearing robes and being a Buddhist monk.

33
00:03:39,003 --> 00:03:43,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And in some cases, like I'm even thinking, if you just look purely at the lifestyle,

34
00:03:45,406 --> 00:04:03,857
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see certain people as, you know, how it's not monks, but you're just living certain kind of lifestyles that are quite solitary or quite, eh... kind of remote and... Quieter, less crowded, less distracting.

35
00:04:03,877 --> 00:04:04,417
[SPEAKER_01]: No, definitely.

36
00:04:04,878 --> 00:04:11,902
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think what is pretty much, probably, the main thing here is the intention of the person doing it.

37
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:15,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, you know, we can... you can have argument for both sides.

38
00:04:15,991 --> 00:04:17,591
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, no, it's still better to be a monk.

39
00:04:18,692 --> 00:04:24,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can say, well, no, see, if my household life is actually quieter, more secluded, more remote, it's better to be that.

40
00:04:24,974 --> 00:04:28,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, in and of itself, it's not a decisive factor.

41
00:04:28,415 --> 00:04:32,736
[SPEAKER_01]: What is decisive factor is your reasons behind choosing to do whatever you choose to do.

42
00:04:33,456 --> 00:04:34,757
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have people who

43
00:04:35,597 --> 00:04:43,863
[SPEAKER_01]: might have a very good lay conditions, quiet, remote, not crowded, plenty of time to practice.

44
00:04:44,003 --> 00:04:51,708
[SPEAKER_01]: However, all of their life, that life, is still rooted in simply being afraid to let go of your environment.

45
00:04:52,368 --> 00:04:56,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say the environment is externally extremely suitable for practice of dhamma.

46
00:04:56,411 --> 00:04:57,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally, it isn't.

47
00:04:58,013 --> 00:05:03,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's all based on the intention of you refusing to give up, out of fear, or whatever it is.

48
00:05:04,319 --> 00:05:10,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Equally, you can have somebody giving everything up, in like a finger snap, committing, unwavering determination to become a monk.

49
00:05:11,506 --> 00:05:19,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, all it's rooted in this quite common view that people have, which is, oh, as long as I become a monk, join the monastery, wear the robes, that's it.

50
00:05:19,894 --> 00:05:22,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice will, from then onwards, be done by the environment.

51
00:05:23,571 --> 00:05:28,893
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is get there, and then I'm sort of safe, safe, or something like that.

52
00:05:29,293 --> 00:05:30,294
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also not the case.

53
00:05:30,354 --> 00:05:30,954
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

54
00:05:31,094 --> 00:05:34,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Entire ordination there is rooted in completely wrong reason.

55
00:05:35,316 --> 00:05:39,438
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the intentions behind doing it is probably the most important, to be clear about that.

56
00:05:39,838 --> 00:05:48,361
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you would know why you're not ordaining, and you would know why you're staying a layman, or you would know why you're not staying a layman, and why you're ordaining.

57
00:05:48,721 --> 00:05:52,663
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't use one to kind of avoid the other, or cover responsibility for it.

58
00:05:56,167 --> 00:06:13,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what's interesting also, like, you become a Buddhist monk, and now, there's all sorts of things you don't do, or you do, and that are sort of automatically justified in your certain environment, because now you're a Buddhist monk.

59
00:06:14,401 --> 00:06:15,722
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something I find interesting.

60
00:06:15,762 --> 00:06:24,009
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, whereas, if you're not a Buddhist monk, as a layperson, doing things like, for example, practicing sense restraint,

61
00:06:25,910 --> 00:06:42,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Spending time in seclusion, not engaging in all sorts of things, it's... you really have to be sure about why you're doing them, or you have to work harder in a way to make it clear to yourself why you're doing them, instead of, I'm a monk and this is what monks do.

62
00:06:44,401 --> 00:06:45,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I meant.

63
00:06:45,762 --> 00:06:48,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that can become an excuse then.

64
00:06:49,493 --> 00:06:59,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Then people, the monks, would then be practicing sense restraint, keeping the rules, adhering to all sorts of observances, out of a sense of duty, not out of a sense of that, you know, taking responsibility for it.

65
00:07:01,162 --> 00:07:08,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that means, as soon as that environment changes, your context will be compromised.

66
00:07:08,809 --> 00:07:09,729
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't want that.

67
00:07:09,929 --> 00:07:12,551
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, if you have any wisdom, you realize, no, my practice should be

68
00:07:13,441 --> 00:07:22,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Developing me in a way that where even my circumstances change, even if I end up in a hospital, or a monastery gets destroyed, or I have to whatever, internally, composure will not change.

69
00:07:22,966 --> 00:07:28,829
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not depend on these circumstances that are quite, you know, tailored and protective of certain sets and routines.

70
00:07:29,450 --> 00:07:33,912
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that kind of allow me to sort of just, you know, stop thinking about it.

71
00:07:34,853 --> 00:07:37,594
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, that's completely misguided.

72
00:07:38,114 --> 00:07:41,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

73
00:07:41,736 --> 00:07:42,597
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the...

74
00:07:46,338 --> 00:07:59,001
[SPEAKER_00]: It should be that it is the choice to live a certain lifestyle that kind of makes you a monk, rather than you live a certain lifestyle just because...

75
00:07:59,021 --> 00:08:08,104
[SPEAKER_01]: But the difference between, like, kind of formally doing it is, again, it is something that you can sort of... that you can't really lie to yourself.

76
00:08:08,124 --> 00:08:09,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if, say, if you're not

77
00:08:11,298 --> 00:08:19,968
[SPEAKER_01]: If you say, oh, I don't want to ordain, because, you know, I have a good environment here, and I have a, you know, secluded property, and so on, and I can live as a layman, restrained in practice, fine.

78
00:08:20,789 --> 00:08:28,257
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, more often than not, there will be rooted in some sort of fear, and still having a thread of safety, like, well, if it doesn't work out, I can still do this.

79
00:08:29,221 --> 00:08:33,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, actually formally ordaining will cut that thread.

80
00:08:35,910 --> 00:08:38,873
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're honest, that would be obvious, and then you would know whether you should or not.

81
00:08:39,294 --> 00:08:44,700
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you genuinely know what the practice is, genuinely just need the seclusion environment to amplify it,

82
00:08:45,361 --> 00:08:53,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, again, you wouldn't... then you would... you could stay a layman or change your lifestyle, as in layman not formally ordained, and still not live like a householder either.

83
00:08:54,725 --> 00:08:58,326
[SPEAKER_01]: But for that, already, you know, some wisdom is quite necessary.

84
00:08:58,346 --> 00:09:02,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Some authenticity and transparency would be mandatory.

85
00:09:03,048 --> 00:09:06,609
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could probably say it's mandatory in all cases, because if you're not...

86
00:09:06,669 --> 00:09:07,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes.

87
00:09:07,049 --> 00:09:12,972
[SPEAKER_01]: ...from the beginning, then becoming a monk will not necessarily make you more... Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

88
00:09:12,992 --> 00:09:13,932
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I can...

89
00:09:16,906 --> 00:09:36,456
[SPEAKER_00]: To paraphrase, it's... is it about what will make me give up the thing that I would be holding on to or what would be putting me into... what would be putting me into the situation that forces me to practice more seriously?

90
00:09:36,476 --> 00:09:37,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

91
00:09:37,537 --> 00:09:41,639
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes, like, that's obviously if things would be clear on that level.

92
00:09:41,659 --> 00:09:43,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes there might be a bit of both.

93
00:09:45,605 --> 00:09:52,867
[SPEAKER_01]: And in those cases, you just need to kind of try and think like, okay, okay, in the long run.

94
00:09:53,107 --> 00:10:01,109
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you know, I'm afraid to give up a household life, but at the same time, I'm afraid if I don't become a monk, I can stay a householder.

95
00:10:01,189 --> 00:10:03,369
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do here?

96
00:10:03,629 --> 00:10:07,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, don't cover up that situation.

97
00:10:09,740 --> 00:10:14,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Endure it without necessarily making your current household environment worse.

98
00:10:15,282 --> 00:10:20,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sometimes people are like, oh, I want to ordain, but if I don't ordain, I have to get married.

99
00:10:20,884 --> 00:10:21,944
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, no.

100
00:10:22,804 --> 00:10:27,026
[SPEAKER_01]: You can still live and practice the dhamma accordingly, even without wearing robes.

101
00:10:27,886 --> 00:10:29,967
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to go the other way.

102
00:10:30,699 --> 00:10:34,240
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it feels like it's either or, means, okay, well, that's really the problem.

103
00:10:34,601 --> 00:10:36,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that you're not a monk yet, or that you're still a householder.

104
00:10:36,782 --> 00:10:42,924
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that you can't, like, restrain yourself and sustain that that middle.

105
00:10:43,764 --> 00:10:46,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, yes, you don't need to become a monk, but that doesn't mean, oh, but I can...

106
00:10:46,986 --> 00:10:56,830
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because I can't become a monk, for some reason, that means I can now engage in sensuality without any kind of responsibility or fault.

107
00:10:57,725 --> 00:10:58,085
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's...

108
00:10:58,205 --> 00:11:00,047
[SPEAKER_01]: I think I even did that talk once.

109
00:11:00,127 --> 00:11:06,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, even if dhamma is your goal, even if you're not a monk, there is no excuse to go to sensuality.

110
00:11:06,932 --> 00:11:07,593
[SPEAKER_01]: It just isn't.

111
00:11:08,193 --> 00:11:15,839
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is something mistaken, like, no, I really want to become a monk, but, you know, some say, like, I can't get the visa, or something like that, or my mother's sick, I have to look after her.

112
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:19,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Some externally valid reason that currently prevents me from becoming a monk.

113
00:11:19,822 --> 00:11:23,065
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the meantime, oh, yeah, I'll find a partner and marry and so on.

114
00:11:23,085 --> 00:11:23,585
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, no

115
00:11:28,425 --> 00:11:39,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because, I mean, let's say, for example, let's say you had the suttas, or you had access to teachings, but there were no order of monks.

116
00:11:40,434 --> 00:11:40,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

117
00:11:40,614 --> 00:11:44,637
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd still have to... you'd be forced to actually try and do something yourself about it.

118
00:11:44,837 --> 00:11:45,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

119
00:11:45,698 --> 00:11:54,725
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking... it reminded me of Kierkegaard, actually, the Christian philosopher, that he, you know, his life... Well, that was exactly the decision he made.

120
00:11:54,765 --> 00:11:55,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

121
00:11:55,205 --> 00:11:56,827
[SPEAKER_01]: That was exactly the point that he arrived at.

122
00:11:56,867 --> 00:11:57,727
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, wait a minute.

123
00:11:58,968 --> 00:12:03,552
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to be an authentic philosopher, true to the pursuit of wisdom.

124
00:12:04,659 --> 00:12:12,846
[SPEAKER_01]: And at the same time, I'm getting engaged in planning a family and a government opposition and work, whatever was lined up for him, and then he realized, well, no.

125
00:12:13,847 --> 00:12:15,929
[SPEAKER_01]: If I want to do this, I have to say no to this.

126
00:12:16,669 --> 00:12:17,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Many of them wouldn't.

127
00:12:17,670 --> 00:12:27,338
[SPEAKER_01]: Many of them would have been quite content in perpetuating the idea that they are here in pursuit of truth and clear philosophy, while doing very mundane, sensual things.

128
00:12:28,099 --> 00:12:30,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Because many of them would not have been honest, as honest as he was.

129
00:12:31,683 --> 00:12:38,404
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, he broke off the engagement, gave everything up, and then just continued pursuing wisdom in a very painful manner for the rest of his life.

130
00:12:39,144 --> 00:12:40,845
[SPEAKER_01]: But he didn't... at least he didn't compromise.

131
00:12:41,745 --> 00:12:42,965
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's quite commendable.

132
00:12:44,985 --> 00:12:47,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and somebody who does that, like for him, he was really...

133
00:12:47,906 --> 00:12:50,526
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking about it, he was really, you know, alone.

134
00:12:50,586 --> 00:12:58,568
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, he had nobody else who was kind of confirming or knows his external structure to tell him that what he was doing was the right thing.

135
00:12:58,588 --> 00:12:58,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

136
00:13:13,278 --> 00:13:21,822
[SPEAKER_00]: On the subject also, it reminded me of that, again, it's something that I'd read often in the suttas, but had not paid that much attention to, like when he says,

137
00:13:25,718 --> 00:13:32,204
[SPEAKER_00]: I did not go forth from this household life for the sake of robes, bowl, medicine for the sick, and so on.

138
00:13:33,205 --> 00:13:39,331
[SPEAKER_00]: I went forth because I was pursued by suffering old age and death, and I thought...

139
00:13:40,332 --> 00:13:49,100
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, I used to kind of think of that in the sense of people just kind of get greedy for these things, or these kind of external whatever.

140
00:13:49,916 --> 00:14:08,728
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can also be that you're, you know, you have all of these things that you have to get and do and accomplish and go to this place as a monk and everything, that it can kind of end up obscuring your basic reason for doing those things in the first place.

141
00:14:08,948 --> 00:14:10,709
[SPEAKER_01]: And result in complacency then.

142
00:14:11,690 --> 00:14:12,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, eh...

143
00:14:13,892 --> 00:14:34,905
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you have a big community, big place, all registered, everything fine, institution, all settled, you know, supporters, you do your duties, you teach, whatever else, and you go through these routines, and, again, it's... it's all going to be rooted in just establishing that external sense of safety, which is no different than a... that's the whole point of a household life.

144
00:14:35,426 --> 00:14:39,808
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although you're wearing the robe, the principles of a household life still apply to you.

145
00:14:41,369 --> 00:14:42,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the...

146
00:14:43,272 --> 00:14:48,433
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that's for everyone to see for themselves.

147
00:14:48,794 --> 00:14:57,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, you could have a relatively accurate guess and speculation about other people's motives, if you see how they behave and so on.

148
00:14:57,576 --> 00:15:02,337
[SPEAKER_01]: But overall, fundamentally, it does come down to every individual for himself.

149
00:15:02,977 --> 00:15:03,758
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to know that.

150
00:15:03,778 --> 00:15:04,978
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to be clear about it.

151
00:15:05,558 --> 00:15:09,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, it's not necessarily, in and of itself, a bad thing.

152
00:15:09,841 --> 00:15:13,122
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said to himself, to Mahākassapa, he said, why do you live in the hard forest?

153
00:15:13,142 --> 00:15:13,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Come with me.

154
00:15:14,243 --> 00:15:22,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Plenty of food, plenty of nice lodgings, plenty of fancy silk robes or whatever else.

155
00:15:22,108 --> 00:15:23,668
[SPEAKER_01]: But he said, no, no, no, I actually prefer this.

156
00:15:24,389 --> 00:15:25,049
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to do it.

157
00:15:25,089 --> 00:15:26,210
[SPEAKER_01]: So, externally, yeah.

158
00:15:26,948 --> 00:15:34,552
[SPEAKER_01]: You might think, you must not, never live in these suitable circumstances or environment, but that's, that's not guaranteed.

159
00:15:34,612 --> 00:15:38,094
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not a, that's not a sure symptom of an underlying problem.

160
00:15:38,974 --> 00:15:43,717
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes equally, yeah, people can go and live in the rough forests and so on, for wrong reasons.

161
00:15:44,417 --> 00:15:55,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Having said that, rough forests, even for wrong reasons, are less likely to give rise to unwholesome states than the soft, pleasant, agreeable, safe conditions would.

162
00:15:57,173 --> 00:16:00,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is your sense of safety?

163
00:16:00,174 --> 00:16:01,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

164
00:16:01,415 --> 00:16:08,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like, yeah, are you still sort of taking it for granted that you... because you shouldn't have a sense of safety upon anything.

165
00:16:08,599 --> 00:16:14,842
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the... like, when the monk... when the Buddha says in the suttas, when the monk realizes, nothing is worth holding on to.

166
00:16:15,402 --> 00:16:17,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing is worth acquiring in regarding his mind.

167
00:16:18,062 --> 00:16:19,203
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I'm unsafe.

